'''Ngomburr''', also spelt '''Ngumbur''', is an extinct [[Australian Aboriginal language]]. It has sometimes been assumed to be a dialect of [[Umbugarla language|Umbugarla]],<ref name="AIATSIS"/> but it is poorly attested; the only evidence to go on is that neighbouring peoples reported that it was similar to Umbugarla, as well as some sentences and vocabulary.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hammarström |first=Harald |date=September 2015 |title=Ethnologue 16/17/18th editions: A comprehensive review: Online appendices |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/article/593411 |journal=Language |language=en |volume=91 |issue=3 |pages=s1–s188 |doi=10.1353/lan.2015.0049 |issn=1535-0665|hdl=11858/00-001M-0000-0029-1D58-0 |hdl-access=free |url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Breen |first=Gavan (collector) |date=1981-09-24 |title=Ngurmbur vocabulary and sentences |url=https://catalog.paradisec.org.au/collections/GB50/items/001?files_page=1&files_per_page=24 |access-date=2024-11-12 |website=PARADISEC Catalog |doi=10.4225/72/5703E9BD993EA}}</ref> It was spoken to the west of the [[South Alligator River]], between the Ga'baarlgu and the [[South Alligator River]], in [[Kakadu, Northern Territory]].<ref name="AIATSIS"/>

There were two speakers recorded in 1975, and one in 1981, but none since then, on the [[AUSTLANG]] database.<ref name="AIATSIS"/> The last speaker, Butcher Knight, died sometime between 1987 and 2001.

